* [33mcommit 8db07afcbd3e80a2d45e990d9fdd2726319df87f[m
[31m|[m Author: kvit-s <info@kvit.app>
[31m|[m Date:   Mon Sep 21 21:06:49 2026 -0500
[31m|[m 
[31m|[m     Take a picture's height from its file too
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     An image block already draws at the width its file says the picture is,
[31m|[m     measured from the file's header rather than read back out of the decoded
[31m|[m     image. Its height was still read back out of the decoded image, so a row
[31m|[m     holding a picture stood 160 px tall — the placeholder tile height —
[31m|[m     until the decode finished, and again every time the list recycled the
[31m|[m     row, since a pooled row drops its pixmap and has nothing of its own left
[31m|[m     to measure.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     The list places every row from the height the row above it reports, so
[31m|[m     each picture that arrived moved the rest of the document under the
[31m|[m     reader, and a scroll that recycled a picture's row did it twice more.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     The height now comes from the same measurement as the width, in both the
[31m|[m     editor's ImageBlock and the read-only picture, which keeps the two
[31m|[m     agreeing on shape as they already agree on width. A picture loaded over
[31m|[m     the network cannot be measured and still answers from the decoded image;
[31m|[m     the tile height stands until either can say.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     tests/test_documentview.cpp pools a row that is drawing a 360x240
[31m|[m     picture and asks it for its geometry with the picture dropped. Before
[31m|[m     this it answered 160 px tall.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
[31m|[m     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01NkdCZYLXxCwj1wuX4dZwLW
[31m|[m 
[31m|[m  CHANGELOG.md                |  6 [32m++++++[m
[31m|[m  qml/ImageBlock.qml          | 17 [32m++++++++++++++[m[31m---[m
[31m|[m  qml/ReadOnlyPicture.qml     | 22 [32m+++++++++++++++++[m[31m-----[m
[31m|[m  tests/test_documentview.cpp | 33 [32m+++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++[m
[31m|[m  4 files changed, 70 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)
[31m|[m 
* [33mcommit 3243a3483dc5e57ce61d60637600ca2b1b9fddac[m
[31m|[m Author: kvit-s <info@kvit.app>
[31m|[m Date:   Mon Sep 21 18:11:57 2026 -0500
[31m|[m 
[31m|[m     Scroll the document by as much as the wheel was turned
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     A QQuickFlickable handles the wheel itself, and measured through the
[31m|[m     shipped shell — sending it wheel events and sampling where the view went
[31m|[m     — what it does with them is two problems.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     It moves too little. One notch, 120 units of angleDelta, moved the view
[31m|[m     25 px. The editor sets prose at 14 px on a line height of 1.3, so a line
[31m|[m     is about 18 px and a notch is not quite a line and a half; three lines,
[31m|[m     which is what the desktop's own wheelScrollLines setting asks for, would
[31m|[m     be 55. A 616 px window took about two dozen notches to turn over one
[31m|[m     screenful.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     And it smears a small delta over a third of a second. A wheel that
[31m|[m     reports finer than one notch at a time — a Logitech MX Master, and
[31m|[m     anything else whose wheel spins freely, over libinput's high-resolution
[31m|[m     scroll axis — sends fractions of 120. Given 15 units the flickable moved
[31m|[m     9 px, delivered as a decay running for 300 ms: 3.1, 1.9, 0.8, 1.1, 0.7,
[31m|[m     0.3, 0.4, 0.2 and then a tail of movements below a tenth of a pixel. A
[31m|[m     spin sends those faster than they decay, so what arrives is overlapping
[31m|[m     decay curves rather than motion. A hundred of them over half a second —
[31m|[m     twelve and a half notches of rotation — moved the view 94 px, under a
[31m|[m     sixth of a screen.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     qml/WheelScroller.qml takes the wheel before the flickable sees it,
[31m|[m     converts the rotation to a distance in text lines, and adds it to a
[31m|[m     target that a frame-synchronised chase closes on. Every event
[31m|[m     contributes its whole distance whether or not a frame was drawn between
[31m|[m     it and the next, and a burst adds up instead of each event replacing the
[31m|[m     last. Over the same events: a notch moves 54.6 px, one fine step 6.8 px
[31m|[m     over 140 ms, and the spin that moved 94 px moves 682 — which is 12.5
[31m|[m     times 54.6, the rotation that was asked for.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Three things the wiring needs. Typewriter mode animates contentY, and an
[31m|[m     animation between the write and the value means the scroller never reads
[31m|[m     back what it wrote and concludes somebody else has the view, so the
[31m|[m     Behavior stands down while the wheel is turning. The scrollbar handle is
[31m|[m     drawn only while the flickable reports itself moving, which a scroll
[31m|[m     driven from outside it does not, so the bar is told about this instead.
[31m|[m     And an editor embedded as a box that grows with its content has nothing
[31m|[m     to scroll, so it declines the wheel rather than eating its host's.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     What this is not. The cost of the QML work per scroll step was measured
[31m|[m     across note sizes and does not grow with them: 0.09 ms median at 30
[31m|[m     blocks, 0.40 at 500, 0.22 at 2000, and one or two new row measurements
[31m|[m     per step even at 6000. The virtualization and the height table are not
[31m|[m     what made scrolling jagged.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
[31m|[m     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KmJjd49uLkKNbSWxCSXJj3
[31m|[m 
