* [33mcommit 238e103f1f0ba21a3457e2890b9bd242fe43a5d2[m
[31m|[m Author: kvit-s <info@kvit.app>
[31m|[m Date:   Sun Sep 13 14:25:27 2026 -0500
[31m|[m 
[31m|[m     search: stop a vault switch from deadlocking the two index threads
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Switching vaults hung the whole application, about one switch in four on a
[31m|[m     loaded machine and never on an idle one, and the stack was the same every
[31m|[m     time. The main thread sat in CollectionSearchIndex::openForRoot() on the
[31m|[m     semaphore of a blocking-queued call into the write worker; the write worker
[31m|[m     sat in SearchIndexDb::close(), blocked on a SQLite mutex at
[31m|[m     QSqlDatabase::removeDatabase(); the read worker sat on a different mutex
[31m|[m     inside the same driver. Nothing timed out, so the window was gone for the rest
[31m|[m     of the session.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     The two workers hold separate connections to one database file, and detaching
[31m|[m     one of them is not a private act: removeDatabase() takes Qt's process-wide
[31m|[m     connection registry lock and destroys the driver underneath it, and destroying
[31m|[m     the driver enters SQLite, which holds locks that the other connection's
[31m|[m     running statement also holds. Reached from two threads at once the two get
[31m|[m     taken in opposite orders. It needs the reader's last statement to still be
[31m|[m     running when the close begins, which is why it is load-dependent and why the
[31m|[m     consequence is a permanent hang rather than a slow switch.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Three things change, and all three are needed.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     A file's connections now say what they are doing, and SearchConnectionGate --
[31m|[m     one object per database file, shared by every connection to it -- keeps the
[31m|[m     two kinds apart. Running a statement is shared use, so the read and write
[31m|[m     connections still overlap, which is what two connections and WAL are for.
[31m|[m     Opening or closing is exclusive: it waits for every statement in flight on
[31m|[m     that file and holds new ones off until it is done. A close therefore waits for
[31m|[m     the readers of that index instead of competing with them inside the driver.
[31m|[m     Connections are also asked for with QSqlDatabase::database(name, false) now:
[31m|[m     the default form reopens a closed connection while holding the registry's read
[31m|[m     lock, which is the other half of the same inversion, and every connection here
[31m|[m     is opened explicitly anyway.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     openForRoot() no longer blocks. It posts the open to the two workers and
[31m|[m     reports on openFinished() and usableChanged(), so a search thread that stops
[31m|[m     answering costs the search rather than the session. The calls that still wait
[31m|[m     -- the rebuild that recovers a degraded index, the blocking close, revisionOf
[31m|[m     and teardown -- are bounded by workerReplyTimeoutMs() and report failure. A
[31m|[m     teardown that times out leaves the thread running rather than destroying a
[31m|[m     QThread that is still going, which would abort the process.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Order comes from the worker queues rather than from waiting. The close of the
[31m|[m     vault being left, the open of the next one and every write in between are
[31m|[m     posted to the same two threads and delivered in the order they were posted, so
[31m|[m     a switch arriving while the previous vault is still closing waits behind that
[31m|[m     close on the search side. The write connection's open is posted first, which
[31m|[m     is what lets a reconcile issued in the same breath land on the new vault's
[31m|[m     database instead of being dropped for arriving early; the read connection lets
[31m|[m     go at once and reattaches only when the writer reports the file is good, since
[31m|[m     the writer owns rebuilding.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     The feed no longer decides whether a root is served by reading isUsable() off
[31m|[m     a call that has not finished. It records the root it asked for and asks the
[31m|[m     index which root it is serving, so a failed open is still visible to the next
[31m|[m     sync.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Tests. testClosingWaitsForTheReadersOfThatFile parks a query inside its scan
[31m|[m     through the cancellation token and asserts the close does not return until the
[31m|[m     query finishes; it fails within a second without the gate.
[31m|[m     testSwitchingRootsUnderQueryLoadCompletesEveryTime runs a hundred switches
[31m|[m     with the read thread busy, each completing inside a bounded time, and asserts
[31m|[m     structurally that the switch cannot have opened anything before returning --
[31m|[m     which a blocking open fails whatever the clock says.
[31m|[m     testWorkQueuedDuringAnOpenReachesTheNewVault covers the ordering that the
[31m|[m     asynchronous open depends on. testAWedgedSearchThreadLeavesTheWindowUsable
[31m|[m     parks both search threads for good and shows the vault still switching, the
[31m|[m     notes still listed, the search degrading and the session still closing.
[31m|[m     
[31m|[m     Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
[31m|[m     Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_011zrE1PkdMPzdv1JiUtTmGM
